1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,824
Tell a roomful of people that your organization served 1,200 individuals last year, and watch what happens: nothing.

2
00:00:06,824 --> 00:00:12,001
The number slides right off, because we are not built to feel a thousand people at once.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:13,413
We're built to feel one.

4
00:00:13,413 --> 00:00:21,590
This lesson is about the impact story: how to make a stranger lean in, and how to do it in a way the person in the story would be proud of.

5
00:00:21,590 --> 00:00:26,648
The impact story takes the statistic and makes it a person someone can actually feel.

6
00:00:26,648 --> 00:00:27,838
One life, told well.

7
00:00:27,838 --> 00:00:35,990
It's the single most powerful tool in nonprofit communications, more persuasive than any data point, any brochure, any mission statement.

8
00:00:35,990 --> 00:00:38,383
It's also the tool most easily misused.

9
00:00:38,383 --> 00:00:41,452
There's a name for getting it wrong: poverty porn.

10
00:00:41,452 --> 00:00:47,650
Storytelling that dwells on suffering, that crops a life down to its bleakest frame to open a wallet.

11
00:00:47,650 --> 00:00:56,610
So this lesson treats craft and ethics as one subject, because the most moving story and the most respectful one are almost always the same story.

12
00:00:56,610 --> 00:01:00,746
Three principles keep a story on the right side of the line.

13
00:01:00,746 --> 00:01:04,743
Dignity: the person is a full human being, not a category.

14
00:01:04,743 --> 00:01:11,085
Agency: they are the actor in their own story; your organization is the guide, not the hero.

15
00:01:11,085 --> 00:01:15,083
And consent: informed, specific, documented, freely given.

16
00:01:15,083 --> 00:01:20,390
One test holds all three: would the person be proud of how they're portrayed?

17
00:01:20,390 --> 00:01:22,594
The difference shows up in the grammar.

18
00:01:22,594 --> 00:01:27,342
We lifted Maria out of poverty makes your organization the hero and Maria the cargo.

19
00:01:27,342 --> 00:01:32,938
With childcare she could finally rely on, Maria finished her degree makes Maria the hero who acted.

20
00:01:32,938 --> 00:01:34,803
Write the person doing the verbs.

21
00:01:34,803 --> 00:01:41,190
It's more ethical, and it's more persuasive: donors want to fund a real person's hard-won progress, not a rescue.

22
00:01:41,190 --> 00:01:43,183
And consent is not a signature.

23
00:01:43,183 --> 00:01:53,856
It's a conversation that answers real questions: where will this appear, will we use your name or a pseudonym, your photo or none, and can you change your mind later?

24
00:01:53,856 --> 00:01:56,556
The person can say no, or yes with limits.

25
00:01:56,556 --> 00:01:59,899
And their access to services can never depend on it.

26
00:01:59,899 --> 00:02:05,750
Get it in writing, but the form is the record of the conversation, not a substitute for it.

27
00:02:05,750 --> 00:02:09,515
With the ethics in place, the structure is three moves.

28
00:02:09,515 --> 00:02:16,086
Situation: where the person was, told with dignity, honest about hardship without exploiting it.

29
00:02:16,086 --> 00:02:24,163
Intervention: what your organization actually did, specifically, because that's the section funders read most closely.

30
00:02:24,163 --> 00:02:27,586
Transformation: what changed, concrete and honest.

31
00:02:27,586 --> 00:02:29,845
And resist the fairy-tale ending.

32
00:02:29,845 --> 00:02:36,690
The partial story is more credible than the miracle, and it respects the truth of the person's life.

33
00:02:36,690 --> 00:02:38,688
Then pair the story with data.

34
00:02:38,688 --> 00:02:45,214
Maria's story is one of many: last year, 84% of parents in this program secured stable employment.

35
00:02:45,214 --> 00:02:47,212
The story earns the attention.

36
00:02:47,212 --> 00:02:49,010
The number earns the trust.

37
00:02:49,010 --> 00:02:54,269
So: situation, intervention, transformation, with the person doing the verbs.

38
00:02:54,269 --> 00:02:57,684
Dignity, agency, and consent from the first draft.

39
00:02:57,684 --> 00:03:01,919
And a story bank so the stories reach you while they're fresh.

40
00:03:01,919 --> 00:03:19,130
The consent form, the story worksheet, and the story bank guide are all in this course, Writing Impact Stories That Move People, and BeMo's Impact Story template turns one strong story into a grant section, an appeal, and a social post in an afternoon.
